<region region-id="343" left="18.00" top="6738.61" width="572.00" height="22.00" align-x="18.00" align-y="6753.61" show-border="false" show-highlight="false" is-protected="true" z-order="0" background-color="inherit" tag="">
<text use-page-width="false" push-down="false" lock-width="true"><p style="Heading 1" margin-left="inherit" margin-right="inherit" text-indent="inherit" text-align="inherit" list-style-type="inherit" tabs="inherit">9. Resultant bending axis (strain compatibility at the demand angle)</p></text>
</region>
<region region-id="344" left="18.00" top="6766.61" width="572.00" height="56.00" align-x="18.00" align-y="6776.61" show-border="false" show-highlight="false" is-protected="true" z-order="0" background-color="inherit" tag="">
<text use-page-width="false" push-down="false" lock-width="true"><p style="Normal" margin-left="inherit" margin-right="inherit" text-indent="inherit" text-align="inherit" list-style-type="inherit" tabs="inherit">The demand moment is resisted about the axis of the resultant moment. The curve is swept again with the section rotated by the folded angle alpha (0 to 45 degrees, the square being symmetric); the demand ray from the origin meets the phi-curve at (Pr, Mr) and D/C = Mu / Mr along the ray. The angle is the notebook&#x27;s; tan(alpha) = M_min / M_max proves it.</p></text>
</region>
<region region-id="345" left="18.00" top="6828.61" width="209.86" height="21.00" align-x="18.00" align-y="6839.61" show-border="false" show-highlight="false" is-protected="true" z-order="0" background-color="inherit" tag="">
